<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/expert/real-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Real analysis re-derives calculus with total honesty. The school version of the derivative
    ("slope of the tangent") hides questions a professional must answer: what <em>is</em> a real
    number? What does "limit" mean precisely? When can functions be swapped, integrated,
    differentiated? Analysis answers with one weapon: the <strong>ε–δ definition</strong> —
    quantified statements where every challenge (choose any ε) has a guaranteed reply (here's a δ).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a sequence converging to e — in analysis, the limit is defined so that 'getting arbitrarily close' means exactly something" widget="plotter" config={eSeqCfg} />

  <p>
    The sequence aₙ = (1 + 1/n)ⁿ climbs toward e ≈ 2.71828 and never arrives. Analysis makes
    "never arrives but converges" precise: for every tolerance ε you demand, there is a point N
    after which all terms are within ε of e. No hand-waving, no "approaches".
  </p>

  <h2>The load-bearing concepts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Completeness of ℝ:</strong> the reals have no holes — every Cauchy sequence converges. This single property, not the derivative, is what separates calculus on ℝ from failure on the rationals (√2 does not live there).</li>
    <li><strong>Uniform vs pointwise convergence:</strong> a limit of continuous functions need not be continuous unless convergence is uniform — the classic trap that ε–δ language exposes.</li>
    <li><strong>Counterexamples are the curriculum:</strong> the function that is 1 on rationals and 0 on irrationals is continuous nowhere; x²sin(1/x²) has an unbounded derivative near 0. Each one marks a boundary where intuition fails.</li>
    <li><strong>Riemann integration, then its limits:</strong> which functions are integrable, and why Lebesgue's 1902 reformulation had to exist for the probability and data-science rungs of this atlas.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Analysis is the subject that turns calculus users into mathematicians: the first time you
    write "Let ε > 0" and mean it, a switch flips.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Abbott, Understanding Analysis; Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis.</p>
</TopicPage>
